modvm
============

modvm is an experimental virtual machine monitor. The Linux x86_64 PC board
uses KVM and supports direct Linux boot, a serial console, and VirtIO PCI
block/network devices.

Build and test
-----------

Requires a C11 compiler, CMake, Ninja, and pthreads. From the project root:

    cmake -S . -B build -G Ninja
    ninja -C build
    ctest --test-dir build --output-on-failure

The executable is build/src/modvm. Tests that create a VM require access to
/dev/kvm. AddressSanitizer, UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer and hardening are enabled
by default. Run outside ptrace-based sandboxes for LeakSanitizer support.

Boot Alpine Linux (x86_64)
-----------

The linux-x86 loader requires Linux boot protocol 2.10 or newer. It loads a
bzImage and optional initramfs directly; no BIOS/UEFI firmware is needed.
The host must provide KVM and the user must have read/write access to /dev/kvm.

On Debian, install curl and libarchive-tools (which provides bsdtar). GNU tar
does not extract ISO images. Download and extract the Alpine Virtual image:

    mkdir -p alpine
    cd alpine
    base=https://dl-cdn.alpinelinux.org/alpine/v3.24/releases/x86_64
    iso=alpine-virt-3.24.1-x86_64.iso
    curl -fLO "$base/$iso"
    curl -fLO "$base/$iso.sha256"
    sha256sum -c "$iso.sha256"
    bsdtar -xf "$iso" boot/vmlinuz-virt boot/initramfs-virt
    cd ..

From the project root, start a single-vCPU, 1 GiB live VM:

    ./build/src/modvm \
      -board pc -accel kvm -m 1024 -smp 1 \
      -loader linux-x86 \
      -drive "driver=linux-file,path=alpine/alpine-virt-3.24.1-x86_64.iso,readonly=on" \
      -loader-opts "kernel=alpine/boot/vmlinuz-virt,initrd=alpine/boot/initramfs-virt,append=console=ttyS0,115200 modules=virtio_pci,virtio_blk,loop,squashfs,iso9660"

Keep append= last: its value consumes the remainder of the loader options.
The ISO appears as a VirtIO disk; Alpine discovers its boot partition and
mounts the included modloop. Log in as root (no password). Press Ctrl+A, then X
to stop modvm. This live configuration has no network or persistent writable
disk; changes inside the guest are lost on exit.

The linux-x86 loader requires Linux boot protocol 2.10 or newer. It places
the initrd above the kernel initialization workspace within addressable RAM.
The PC board provides MP 1.4 CPU and interrupt tables. ACPI and RTC are not
implemented. CPU topology is one core and one thread per virtual socket;
-smp selects the number of sockets/vCPUs (up to 254 on this board).

Alpine networking
-----------------
Add -net "driver=linux-tap,ifname=tap0" to the boot command. The host must
connect tap0 to a network providing DHCP/routing. For an existing libvirt
virbr0 network with DHCP/NAT already configured:

    sudo ip link set tap0 master virbr0
    sudo ip link set virbr0 up
    sudo ip link set tap0 up

Then, inside Alpine:

    ip link set lo up
    ip link set eth0 up
    udhcpc -i eth0 -n -q

These host bridge settings are temporary. After a host reboot, create tap0
again if absent, then attach it to the bridge:

    sudo ip tuntap add dev tap0 mode tap user "$USER"

The libvirt network supplying virbr0 must already be active and provide
DHCP/NAT. Restart modvm after rebuilding it.

TAP defaults to a randomly generated locally administered unicast MAC.
For a stable DHCP identity, specify it explicitly:
  -net "driver=linux-tap,ifname=tap0,mac=52:54:00:12:34:56"
Use distinct MACs for interfaces sharing a network. Duplicate MACs within
one VM are rejected.

`raw-x86` loads the file named by `-loader-opts` at GPA 0 and starts real-mode
execution at CS:IP 0000:0000. This is a flat program loader, not a BIOS ROM
reset-vector loader.
